WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.200
Cyber incidents create continuity problems when systems, visibility, communications, identity, or trust can no longer be relied upon.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.738
Ransomware may disable HMIs and servers.

00:00:12.738 --> 00:00:15.804
Destructive activity may affect configurations.

00:00:15.804 --> 00:00:24.060
Loss of remote access may isolate support, while loss of monitoring can make continued operation unsafe.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:35.619
Incident response and continuity teams should share one operating picture: affected assets, process state, safe operating limits, evidence, containment options, and recovery priorities.

00:00:35.619 --> 00:00:41.988
Cybersecurity leads investigation; operations and engineering determine physical consequence and safe action.

00:00:41.988 --> 00:00:44.582
Containment choices require care.

00:00:44.582 --> 00:00:53.310
Disconnecting a network path may stop unwanted access but also remove alarms, control, redundancy, or historian data.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:01:01.566
Decisions should follow preplanned authority and consider whether a local fallback or controlled shutdown is available.

00:01:01.566 --> 00:01:10.294
A safe shutdown may be the best continuity action when process integrity or visibility cannot be trusted.

00:01:10.294 --> 00:01:20.437
Plans should define shutdown prerequisites, communications, staffing, restart conditions, and the assets that must remain protected while production is stopped.

00:01:20.437 --> 00:01:25.391
Do not rush restoration because business pressure is high.

00:01:25.391 --> 00:01:31.288
Validate backups, images, controller logic, configurations, credentials, and dependencies before reconnecting.

00:01:31.288 --> 00:01:36.242
Preserve evidence and determine whether the cause remains present.

00:01:36.242 --> 00:01:41.195
Recovery from a compromised state can reintroduce the incident.

00:01:41.195 --> 00:01:48.036
Communicate clearly with leadership, employees, vendors, customers, regulators, and public authorities as required.

00:01:48.036 --> 00:01:54.405
After recovery, update process maps, backups, fallback procedures, security controls, and exercises.

00:01:54.405 --> 00:02:01.245
Continuity succeeds when the incident is contained without sacrificing safe and trustworthy operations.
